1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,839
Ops opens today’s manifest list for vessel
IMO-9312345. Twenty manifests. The SQL log
shows one SELECT for manifests — fine —
then twenty more as the JSON serializer
walks each manifest’s cargo lines.

2
00:00:15,959 --> 00:00:31,559
That pattern is the N+1 problem: 1 query
for the parents plus N queries for
children, one per parent. It shows up
wherever lazy associations meet loops.
Look at this java for a moment. Look at
this java for a moment.

3
00:00:31,679 --> 00:00:42,280
Count it out loud. Twenty manifests →
twenty child selects → twenty-one
statements total. Scale to two hundred
manifests on a busy day and the board
melts. Lazy is not the villain.

4
00:00:42,399 --> 00:00:49,920
Lazy keeps writes and incidental loads
cheap. The villain is navigating lazy
associations across a collection without a
fetch plan.

5
00:00:50,039 --> 00:01:01,560
Open-session-in-view can make this "work"
without LazyInitializationException while
still issuing N+1 SQL — silent latency.
Fix it by loading what you need in fewer
queries.

6
00:01:01,679 --> 00:01:12,560
Join fetch is the clearest teaching fix:
Look at this java for a moment. Look at
this java for a moment. join fetch tells
Hibernate to initialize lines while
loading Manifest.

7
00:01:12,680 --> 00:01:25,360
distinct softens duplicate parent rows
joins can produce in the result list. For
pagination, join fetch of collections is
awkward — the database page of joined rows
is not a page of parent entities.

8
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:39,480
Prefer fetch joins for non-paged detail
lists, or page parent ids first, then
fetch aggregates by those ids. @BatchSize
on the collection reduces N+1 into fewer
batched IN queries when you cannot join-
fetch every path.

9
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:55,480
Entity graphs offer another explicit plan:
@EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"lines"})
on a repository method. A DTO query that
selects exactly the columns the screen
needs avoids hydrating the graph entirely.

10
00:01:55,600 --> 00:02:07,480
Diagnosis before dogma. Enable SQL
logging. Reproduce the manifest list.
Count statements. If you see the repeating
cargo_lines select inside the loop, you
found N+1.

11
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:19,680
Fix with fetch join, entity graph, batch
size, or a projection — not by marking
every association FetchType.EAGER, which
often moves the pain to writes and to
every accidental load.

12
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:28,439
Notice what these read fixes still assume:
an open persistence context spanning the
whole operation. Writes raise a sharper
problem.

13
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:36,400
Decrement yard stock, write a berth
reservation, and append a ledger row —
three inserts/updates that must succeed or
fail together.

14
00:02:36,520 --> 00:02:45,520
If the reservation saves and the ledger
write throws, you cannot leave the harbor
half-updated. Something has to bound that
multi-write unit of work atomically.

15
00:02:45,640 --> 00:02:55,160
That boundary is @Transactional — and it
is where persistence context lifetime,
commit, and rollback become deliberate
instead of accidental.
